cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "On the equation n'' = n and a problem of Erdos and Barbeau on products of prime-reciprocal sums"
abstract: >-
  A structural study of Erdos Problem #307: do finite sets of distinct primes P, Q exist with
  (sum 1/p)(sum 1/q) = 1? The problem is identified with Conjecture 4 of Ufnarovski and Ahlander
  (2003), which states it in the arithmetic-derivative literature, so #307 and the two-cycle
  question n'' = n are the same problem. A barrier is proved: any solution has |P u Q| >= 61 and
  min(prod P, prod Q) > 5.89e58. The bound |P u Q| >= 60 and min(prod P, prod Q) >= 2e56 is machine-checked in Lean 4
  without hypotheses; the level-60 closure behind 61 is established by computation and formalised at a second tier, as
  one theorem modulo a single named statement (Stage A), which is computed twice by independent programs. This appears to
  be the first valid unconditional bound of its kind, the only prior candidate resting on an invalid step. The constructive route is closed for families of any growth
  rate, not merely polynomial ones, which rules out the Thabit/Euler programme that is the classical
  technique for problems of this kind. The known approximation theorem is made effective and given a
  certified witness: disjoint prime sets with |(sum 1/p)(sum 1/q) - 1| = 4.98e-4016, all 69 primes
  proved prime. The near-miss frontier is shown to obey a double-logarithmic law, which recovers the
  barrier constant from an independent direction. The barrier is shown to be uniform in the period,
  so it bounds the whole Ufnarovski-Ahlander cycle conjecture and not only its period-two case, and
  six barriers of the note are identified as one mass-to-size inequality read at different
  constants. The same inequality, read in the size, the ratio, the period, the smallest prime, the
  orbit length and the split, yields a family of barriers that also derives the forced core, the
  ceiling on the largest prime, and the level-59 admissible count. Several other approaches are closed with proofs, and the
  constructive route is given an exact cost accounting. Every level of the problem is shown to be
  finite and effectively decidable: for each pair of cardinalities there are only finitely many
  solutions, so the infinitude of admissible supports above level 59 bounds the search space and not
  the solution set, and the problem reduces to the halting of one explicit search. The largest
  element of a support is bounded by an explicit function of the others, and the 59 smallest are
  bounded at every level, which gives the first open level a stated size. The core formalisation carries
  no non-standard axioms: all 477 probed declarations depend only on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. A separate
  tier (lean/Erdos307/Computational, lean/QNative) adds one native_decide axiom per compiled evaluation, 39 in all, for the
  level-60 closure. The problem itself remains open.
